# Line endings, settled once, because getting them wrong here is not cosmetic.
#
# Contributors run Windows, Linux and macOS against the same files. Without this, git checks text
# out with CRLF on Windows and LF elsewhere, so the *same* file has different bytes depending on
# who cloned it. That produced three separate bugs in one day:
#
#   - `submit` compared a script against the library byte for byte to avoid sending a duplicate.
#     The library copy was CRLF and the outgoing copy LF, so nothing ever matched and every run
#     would have added another stamped copy of the same generator.
#   - Having fixed that comparison, the blob was still uploaded, and a pull request rewrote every
#     line of an unchanged file: 95 insertions and 95 deletions on a no-op.
#   - A findings row is `hash,name`. A stray CR at the end of a line is part of the name as far as
#     any hash is concerned, so a file that round-tripped through the wrong checkout could carry
#     names that do not reproduce their own id.
#
# So the text this project actually reads and writes is LF everywhere, on every platform.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The things whose exact bytes matter, and which must never be touched.
*.exe   binary
*.ids   binary
bin/**  binary

# Findings and submissions are data, not prose: `hash,name` rows that get hashed, compared and
# merged. LF, always, whatever the contributor's platform does with everything else.
submissions/**  text eol=lf
findings/**     text eol=lf

# Generators are copied between `contrib/`, `scripts/` and `scripts/contributed/` and compared
# byte for byte on the way, so they need one spelling.
scripts/**      text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.rs            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
